\f0\fs22 \cf2 The form was yellow, which meant swimming.\
There was a colour for everything. Blue was excursions, green was anything involving a bus, pink was the one that came home four times a year and went back with money in it. Yellow was water. Sarah had been collecting yellow forms since she was five, the way other children collected stickers, and had never once handed one back.\
This one had been on the kitchen table for eleven days.\
She had filled in her half on the first night, in her best handwriting, the slow kind that made her wrist ache. Name: JACOBSON, SARAH. Class. Age. The box that asked what stroke you would be swimming, which she had thought about for a long time and answered honestly, because she did not know, and had written LEARNING, and then crossed it out because it looked like an excuse, and written FREESTYLE, and gone to bed feeling like a liar and a criminal and, underneath that, wonderful.\
She had also filled in the line marked PARENT OR GUARDIAN (BLOCK LETTERS), because it seemed helpful, and because it was one less thing.\
ANSTEY, J.\
Underneath that was a second box, ruled off with a heavier line, and the second box was the problem. The second box was for a doctor.\
Her mother came in at twenty past eight with her coat still on and grey dust in the turn-ups of her trousers, and looked at the form the way you look at a cat that has brought something in.\
\u8220 ?I have to hand it in tomorrow,\u8221 ? Sarah said. \u8220 ?It\u8217 ?s the last day. Mrs Pinder said.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?Did she.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?It\u8217 ?s just the carnival. It\u8217 ?s not even a real thing, it\u8217 ?s in the afternoon and then we go home.\u8221 ?\
Her mother put her bag down and went to the sink and ran the tap and did not drink anything, which was a thing she did.\
Sarah had planned this. She had planned it for eleven days and had a case, and she made it well, because she was a child who made cases and had learned young that the ones you shouted did not work.\
She had been well for four years. Four years, and she had the evidence: no winters, no Tuesdays at the clinic, no plastic chairs, no forms in any other colour. She had not needed a single thing from anybody. She had not been a bother. She had not asked for the trip to the aquarium or the one with the bus, which she had wanted so much that she had not asked, on the calculation that not asking for two would buy her one. This was the one.\
Her mother said, \u8220 ?No.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?You haven\u8217 ?t even \u8212 ?\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?No, Sarah.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?It\u8217 ?s one afternoon.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?I know what it is.\u8221 ?\
And there it was, the whole shape of it, in the length of the kitchen: a woman who would not say why, and a child who could not be told, and a yellow form on the table between them with a heavy line ruled across the middle of it.\
Sarah went at the line. She had worked out that the doctor\u8217 ?s box was the hinge; she had not worked out anything past that.\
\u8220 ?Ellie\u8217 ?s mum signs hers and they never even go. They just sign it. Mrs Pinder said if a parent signs the top part then the \u8212 ?\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?Mrs Pinder is not a doctor.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?Neither are you.\u8221 ?\
Her mother turned round at that, and Sarah felt the small cold satisfaction of having landed one, and then, immediately, the worse feeling underneath it.\
\u8220 ?I\u8217 ?m not sick,\u8221 ? Sarah said, and her voice came out wrong, higher than she wanted. \u8220 ?I\u8217 ?m not sick any more. You know I\u8217 ?m not. You made me not sick.\u8221 ?\
Her mother did not move.\
\u8220 ?So what was the point,\u8221 ? Sarah said, \u8220 ?if I still can\u8217 ?t do anything.\u8221 ?\
It was the closest either of them ever came.\
Her mother looked at her for about three seconds. Whatever went across her face, Sarah did not have the equipment to read it at eleven and would not have another chance. Then it was gone, and her mother said, \u8220 ?Get the bowl.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?I don\u8217 ?t want to.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?Sarah. Get the bowl.\u8221 ?\
So the argument ended, the way arguments in that house ended, which was that one of them had work.}